<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Custom of Paris</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Coutume de Paris</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Custom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> was the body of customary law of the region of Paris, first written down in 1510 and revised in 1580. Made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a> in 1664, it governed property, inheritance, marriage and the seigneurial system in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, survived the British conquest under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec-act">Quebec Act</a> of 1774 and remained the basis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> law until the Civil Code of 1866.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/custom-of-paris.jpg" alt="Custom of Paris" width="320" height="512" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Guy Carleton (died 1808) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Custom_of_Paris_as_Practised_in_Quebec_during_the_French_government.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Custom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> arose from a wider effort to centralise French law. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> had no single law: its regions followed their own blends of jus commune and customary law, and the Custom of Paris was one of 360 uncodified custumals in force in 15th-century France. Because Paris was the capital, its customary law was held in high regard, and it was refined between the 13th and 15th centuries as part of the codification of all French custumals ordered by King Charles VII in the Ordinance of Montil-les-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a> in 1453. The revision of 1580 was ordered by King Henry III after a period of disuse.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Divided into 16 sections, the Custom held 362 articles on family and inheritance, property and debt recovery, and sought to join feudal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-tenure">land tenure</a> to the growing town-based commerce of the Ancien Régime. Property was either movable or immovable, and to encourage trade movables could not be mortgaged. Land was held in allod or in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a>, a fief either as a seigneurie or as a roture, whose holder owed the lord an annual cens and, on a sale, an entry fine (lods et ventes) of a twelfth of the price.</p><p>A married couple held its goods in community of property (communauté de biens). The husband was its head and master, but he needed his wife's consent to dispose of community property, and a widow was protected by dower (douaire). Children of either sex were entitled to a legitime, could not be disinherited and came into their inheritance at 25; a seigneurie passed half to the eldest son, while land held in roture was divided equally. Shaped by the canon-law ban on interest, the Custom forbade notaries to write interest into contracts, except for rentcharges.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The Custom had been part of the justice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> since the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/company-of-one-hundred-associates">Company of One Hundred Associates</a> in 1627, and Article 33 of the charter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-west-india-company">French West India Company</a> introduced it officially in May 1664. At its widest it applied in Canada, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acadia">Acadia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> and Île Royale. By 1760, as applied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a> and Quebec City, it had become the law of Canada and on some points had diverged from its French origins.</p><p>After the British conquest the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-proclamation-of-1763">Royal Proclamation of 1763</a> introduced English common law, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-canadians">French Canadians</a> went on using notaries for their affairs until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec-act">Quebec Act</a> of 1774 restored French private law on property and civil rights, while English law kept its hold over criminal matters.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Custom of Paris” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A Sovereign Council at Quebec, later called the Superior Council, registered laws and served as the highest court, and the <b>Custom of Paris</b> was the civil law of the colony.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-france">New France · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>As a crown colony Saint Lucia had no elected assembly for most of the 19th century, and the <b>Coutume de Paris</b> remained the basis of civil law until a new code in 1879.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-saint-lucia">Colony of Saint Lucia</a></li></ul></section>
